and traceability are technical requirements. If you don't know a system is \"high-risk\" until it's finished, adding logging/traceability retroactively to an architecture that wasn't built for it is a nightmare (and often impossible without total redesign).\n    *   From the perspective of *scientific impact*, ensuring that research results can actually be transitioned into usable, compliant systems increases the real-world utility of the intellectual merit.\n\n    *   Is this \"too bureaucratic\"? Possibly. But as an R&D professional, I know that \"late-stage redesigns\" are a primary killer of scientific momentum and project delivery.\n    *   Does it interfere with the *knowledge frontier*? Not inherently, provided the \"classification memo\" identifies requirements rather than forbidding research. It's about *how* to build it, not *whether* to explore the idea.\n    *   Actually, for a researcher, knowing early if a system falls under Article 12 allows them to bake in the necessary telemetry/logging from the start, which is better for scientific rigor anyway (better data on how the system performs throughout its lifetime).\n\n    *   Rationale: Early identification of high-risk classification prevents catastrophic architectural rework and ensures that technical requirements for traceability are integrated into the research design rather than bolted on. This streamlines the path from intellectual discovery to viable deployment without sacrificing scientific rigor.\n    *   Decision: approve.\n    *   Promise: 85 (High, because while bureaucracy is annoying, \"late-stage redesign\" is worse).\n\n    *   ID: P2969.\n    *   Rationale: Integrated early classification ensures that the technical requirements for logging and traceability are built into the system architecture from the start, avoiding disruptive late-stage redesigns. This allows researchers to focus on scientific merit while ensuring their work remains viable for real-world application.\n    *   Decision: approve.\n    *   Promise: 85.",
