ware approach at the orchestration layer cannot automatically know *what* internal application logic constituted a \"match\" or \"verification\" without some API for the developers to send those logs.\n    *   *However*, having a centralized sink/middleware is vastly superior to each project reinventing the wheel. It provides a standardized path for these specific logs to be sent and stored.\n\n    *   Does it solve Article 12? Partially (infrastructure side) and enables the rest (application side).\n    *   Does it help prototyping speed? Yes, by removing the need for per-project logging infrastructure.\n    *   Is it viable? Very. It's a standard architectural pattern.\n    *   Resource allocation: 1 senior + 2 infra staff is reasonable for a shared service.\n\n    *   Rationale: Standardizing log capture at the orchestration layer significantly reduces the engineering overhead for individual teams and ensures basic traceability by default, though application-specific logs will still require some minimal integration.\n    *   Decision: Approve.\n    *   Promise: High (around 85-90), as it's a low-risk, high-reward infrastructure play that supports agility.",
