nover) or forced market withdrawal. A systemic approach that prevents compliance gaps is a survival strategy.\n    *   *Market Reputation:* Being seen as a leader in \"intellectual rigour,\" \"reproducibility,\" and \"transparency\" enhances reputation, especially with regulators and B2B partners who will need this documentation for their own compliance (downstream).\n    *   *Comparison to Requirements:* While Art 12 focuses heavily on *runtime logs*, Recital 71 (which informs the interpretation of the Act) focuses on the *development record*. The proposal handles the development/provenance side perfectly. It doesn't explicitly mention the runtime event logging required by Art 12(1), but it provides the foundation for the \"technical documentation\" mentioned in Recital 71 and the context needed to interpret those logs.\n\n    *   The proposal shifts compliance from a \"chore at the end\" to a \"byproduct of research.\"\n    *   It reduces the risk of missing data during an audit.\n    *   It improves overall R&D quality (reproducibility).\n\n    *   Rationale: This proposal transforms regulatory record-keeping from a risky post-hoc exercise into a systematic, automated byproduct of the R&D process, directly addressing the documentation requirements of Recital 71 and reducing audit risk. By institutionalizing provenance, we safeguard our market reputation for rigor and ensure survival against strict EU enforcement actions.\n    *   Decision: approve.\n    *   Promise: 95 (Extremely strong because it solves the \"documentation debt\" problem that usually kills AI startups/labs during audits).",
