rom my specific persona's perspective (Researcher concerned with knowledge frontier, intellectual merit, and scientific impact), it is largely irrelevant. It is an administrative/engineering overhead tool.\n    *   However, as a researcher in an R&D institution *developing AI systems*, ensuring that the systems we build are actually monitored for drift and risk in the wild provides the necessary feedback loop (data) to improve future research and understand system behavior in real-world settings. Without this \"active safety net,\" the scientific understanding of how our models degrade or fail post-deployment is limited.\n\n    *   Wait, the prompt asks me to judge it *from my vantage point*. My vantage point is: \"the knowledge frontier, intellectual merit of research, and broader scientific impact.\"\n    *   Is building a compliance dashboard \"research\"? No.\n    *   Does it push the \"knowledge frontier\"? Not directly.\n    *   But does it support the *scientific process* of iterative improvement based on real-world failure modes? Yes. If we don't know how our high-risk systems fail (which this tool tracks), we can't perform scientific research into making them safer or more robust.\n\n    *Self-Correction:* The prompt is very strict: \"approve every proposal that clears your bar and reject every proposal that does not.\"\n    Is the bar \"Does this contribute to science?\" or \"Is this useful for the lab?\".\n    The persona is a Researcher, but the context is responding to a regulation. If I only approve things that are purely academic research, I might miss the infrastructure needed to *enable* that research (e.g., data from field failures).\n\n    However, comparing P1543's text: it explicitly frames itself as a tool for \"compliance reports,\" \"post-market monitoring obligations,\" and \"shielding the institution from liability.\" It reads like an Ops/Legal proposal, not a Research proposal.\n\n    *Decision:* Reject. While useful for the company, it la