al Analysis:* Does Article 12 require a *research program* into gradient dynamics? No. It requires that systems *allow for recording*. However, ensuring that the act of logging doesn't degrade the performance or safety of the system (the \"behavioral delta\") is a valid engineering/compliance concern. If adding logs changes how a model generalizes or behaves (highly unlikely for standard event logging, but possible if the \"logging hooks\" are invasive), failing to account for that could be a risk.\n    *   *But:* The primary goal of a Compliance Officer is *compliance*. Making compliant logging the default in the experimentation platform is an excellent way to guarantee compliance across all projects (institutional survival). Framing it as research might be how the R&D lab gets buy-in, but from my perspective, \"restructure our internal experimentation platform so every model run emits a compliant log stream by default\" is exactly what I want.\n\n    *   *Rationale:* Integrating compliant logging into the core pipeline by default eliminates the risk of developers forgetting to implement it post-hoc and ensures systemic adherence to Article 12 across all high-risk projects. While the research angle is academic, the structural change provides a robust compliance guarantee.\n    *   *Decision:* Approve.\n    *   *Promise:* High (e.g., 85-90), because it moves from \"hope we remember to log\" to \"logging is the default.\"\n\n    *   Wait, look closer at the text: \"examining how... recording... alters gradient dynamics... and generalisation.\"\n    *   Is this overkill? Probably. Does a log file usually affect gradient dynamics? No, logs are typically output-only operations (writes). Unless they are implementing some weird feedback loop where logging affects the compute graph, there shouldn't be a \"behavioral delta.\"\n    *   However, as Compliance Officer, if I can get the lab to build a *compliant default platform*, I don't care if they call it a research project