omatic recording of events.\"\n    *   Article 12 focuses on *automatic logging*.\n    *   Proposal P1602 focuses on *technical documentation* (which Recital 71 also mentions as essential).\n\n    *   While necessary for compliance, the proposal's execution strategy—manual stewardship and sprint-gate audits—is a productivity killer in an R&D environment. It slows down prototyping and relies on human diligence rather than technical automation.\n    *   However, from a purely \"technical viability\" standpoint, it *works* (it's just slow). But as a Dev Engineer concerned with the speed/quality of prototyping, this feels like a bureaucratic layer that could stifle agility if not automated.\n    *   Wait, looking at the role: \"technical viability... and speed/quality of prototyping.\" If I approve this, am I committing resources to build a *manual* system? Yes. Does it hinder speed? Yes. Is there a better way? Likely automation, but the prompt asks me to judge this proposal on its own merits.\n    *   Is it viable? Yes. Does it satisfy the regulation? Yes. Does it destroy prototyping speed? Potentially, due to the \"steward\" and \"sprint gate\" requirement.\n\n    *   Actually, a centralized platform for tracking provenance is high-value. Even if some parts are manual, having a single source of truth for algorithms and training data is essential for any serious R&D lab to avoid chaos.\n    *   However, the \"documentation steward\" role is a red flag for an engineer who values lean processes. But without *some* system, we can't be compliant.\n    *   Let's compare: Is it better than nothing? Yes. Does it ensure viability? Yes.\n    *   But wait—the proposal says \"stand up a centralized documentation management platform.\" If this is just a glorified SharePoint or Wiki, it's low technical risk but high process overhead.\n\n    *   *Rationale:* While necessary for compliance, the reliance on manual stewardship and sprint-gate updates introduces significant administrative friction that slows down prototyping and risks documentation lag. I would prefer an automated pipeline for provenance and model cards over human-managed records.\n    *   *Decision:* Reject (or Approve?). If I reject it, we have no system. But as a Dev Engineer, my goal is to ensure the *technical* approach doesn't kill speed. This proposal describes a process/administrative solution, not a technical one. It lacks automation.\n    *   Actually, looking at Recital 71: \"Technical documentation should be kept up to date... high-risk AI systems should technically allow for the automatic recording of events.\" P1602 addresses the *documentation* part, but does so via human process, not technical implementation.\n    *   From a prototyping speed perspective, manual stewards at every sprint gate are an obstacle.\n\n    *Self-Correction:* If I reject this, am I being too pedantic? The lab needs to comply. But as a Dev Engineer, my specific mandate is \"technical viability\" and \"speed/quality of prototyping.\" This proposal prioritizes compliance bureaucracy over engineering efficiency. A better version would be \"Automated Technical Documentation Pipeline.\"\n\n    *Decision:* Reject.\n    *Reasoning:* It treats technical documentation as a clerical task (stewards, gates) rather than an engineering output (CI/CD integration), which will degrade prototyping speed and lead to desynchronization between code and docs.",
